The JS reference never clears its `satisfied` flag, so once a kinded binding
has been disposed by hand it is treated as answered for the rest of the scope -
even after an assignment gave it a completely different value. Under the
reference `rebindOnce` prints no `close 2` at all: the value the binding ends up
holding is never closed.

That is exactly the pattern std/core/kinds.yoop documents as the informed opt-in
for `let disposable` ("dispose the old value before you overwrite it"), so the
reference leaks on its own recommended idiom. Probed on 2026-08-14 rather than
read off its source; see typecheck/discharge.yoop.

Reproducing a leak to match would not be parity worth having, so this fixture
asserts the bootstrap's behaviour alone. The same call this fixture makes to
`Closer.close(ref r)` before an ORDINARY scope end is asserted against both
compilers in dispose_manual.yoop - it is only the rebind the two disagree about.
